input.script:
export const meta = {
  name: 'queue-components-feature',
  description: 'Implement the queue component tools from the spec, review with five lenses, verify findings adversarially, fix, run task',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Implement', detail: 'one agent builds the feature end to end from the spec' },
    { title: 'Review', detail: 'five independent reviewers, one lens each' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'three skeptics per finding' },
    { title: 'Fix', detail: 'apply confirmed findings, run task' },
  ],
}

const SPEC = '/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-Workspace-Projects---ai-mcp-mcp-yandex-tracker/b5ea5d7d-8dd6-4088-8cd3-d13f4493ad7d/scratchpad/components-spec.md'
const REPO = '/Volumes/Workspace/Projects/_/ai/mcp/mcp-yandex-tracker'

const IMPLEMENT_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    summary: { type: 'string' },
    files_changed: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    deviations: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'anything in the spec not followed, with why' },
    guesses: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'behaviour you had to decide without the spec' },
    task_passed: { type: 'boolean' },
    task_output_tail: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['summary', 'files_changed', 'deviations', 'guesses', 'task_passed', 'task_output_tail'],
}

const FINDINGS_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    findings: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          file: { type: 'string' },
          line: { type: 'integer' },
          title: { type: 'string' },
          description: { type: 'string', description: 'what is wrong, with the evidence (quote the code or the rule)' },
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['high', 'medium', 'low'] },
          suggested_fix: { type: 'string' },
        },
        required: ['file', 'title', 'description', 'severity', 'suggested_fix'],
      },
    },
  },
  required: ['findings'],
}

const VERDICT_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    refuted: { type: 'boolean' },
    reasoning: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['refuted', 'reasoning'],
}

const FIX_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    applied: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    skipped: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { title: { type: 'string' }, reason: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['title', 'reason'] } },
    task_passed: { type: 'boolean' },
    task_output_tail: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['applied', 'skipped', 'task_passed', 'task_output_tail'],
}

const COMMON = `You work in the git repository at ${REPO}, on the checked-out branch feat/queue-components. Do NOT run git commit, git checkout, git stash, git reset or anything that changes branches or history. Do not touch the .env file. Do not make any request to the live Yandex Tracker API. The implementation spec is at ${SPEC} - read it in full first, then AGENTS.md and every file under rules/. Run commands with \`uv run ...\` and \`task\` from the repository root.`

phase('Implement')
log('Implementing the component tools from the spec')
const impl = await agent(
  `${COMMON}

Implement everything the spec describes: types, errors, request funnel change, protocols, client, caching wrappers, context and lifespan wiring, access helpers, params aliases, the five tools, tests, manifest, both READMEs, CHANGELOG, AGENTS.md, rules/tool-naming.md, the server instructions. Follow the repository's existing code style closely - open the neighbouring modules (proto/boards.py, tools/board.py, tools/queue_write.py, tools/issue_write.py checklist tools, caching/client.py, tests/tracker/custom/boards/, tests/tracker/caching/test_boards_protocol.py, tests/mcp/tools/test_queue_write_tools.py) and copy their shape. Read the PR's original code with \`git diff f2764b0 origin/pr/30\` for payload shapes and test ideas, but do not copy it verbatim - it predates the funnel and the rules.

Work until \`task\` passes (format, lint, type check, tests). If a rule test fails (tool naming, description length, README coverage, manifest sync, instructions), fix the cause, never the test - except for the deliberate additions the spec names (TOOL_NAME_ENTITIES, NON_TOOL_NAMES, the tool name lists in test_server_creation.py).

Return the structured report. In task_output_tail put the last 40 lines of the final \`task\` run.`,
  { label: 'implement', phase: 'Implement', schema: IMPLEMENT_SCHEMA, effort: 'high' },
)
if (!impl) throw new Error('implementer returned nothing')
log(`Implementation done, task_passed=${impl.task_passed}, ${impl.files_changed.length} files, ${impl.deviations.length} deviations`)

phase('Review')
const LENSES = [
  {
    key: 'rules',
    prompt: `Lens: the repository's own rules. Check the diff against rules/tool-naming.md, rules/tool-descriptions.md, rules/docs.md, rules/changelog.md and the AGENTS.md checklist "Adding New MCP Tools": tool names and modules, description budget (40-350 chars) and content (entity noun, Russian term, only traps in the second sentence, no internals), parameters shared between create/update as Annotated aliases in mcp/params.py with identical wording, manifest sentences under 160 chars, README/README_ru parity (same sections, order, anchors, rows), CHANGELOG shape, AGENTS.md updated (protocols, module list, categories table, API behaviour bullet), server instructions.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'access',
    prompt: `Lens: access control and configuration. Check every tool against TRACKER_LIMIT_QUEUES (allow-list), TRACKER_READ_ONLY_QUEUES (write=True), TRACKER_READ_ONLY (write tools unregistered), and TOOLS_CACHE_ENABLED: can a component in a restricted queue be read, changed or deleted by id? Does component_update / component_delete make exactly the round trips the spec prescribes (no GET on an unrestricted server when version is given; one GET otherwise)? Does the caching wrapper override every protocol method, and are component reads left uncached as the spec says, with the reason recorded? Is the 404-vs-not-allowed behaviour consistent with the queue tools?`,
  },
  {
    key: 'api',
    prompt: `Lens: correctness against the Tracker API as verified in the spec. Compare the client methods with the recorded live behaviour: URLs, methods, the version query parameter on PATCH, body field spelling (assignAuto on the wire, lead as a login string, queue key on POST), optional fields omitted when None, 412 and 409 mapped to the conflict error, 404 to ComponentNotFound / QueueNotFound, empty update refused before any request, DELETE without version, the Component model (required id/version/name, alias pair for assign_auto, references for queue and lead) and that every request goes through _request()/_read(). Also check that error messages tell an agent what to do next and do not claim things the API does not do.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'tests',
    prompt: `Lens: tests. Check that the test list in the spec is covered and that each test asserts real behaviour (URL, params, body, headers, parsed fields, error types, call counts) rather than only "not isError". Check the testing rules in AGENTS.md: pytest asyncio auto, aioresponses + RequestCapture for the client, AsyncMock(spec=...) for protocols, type hints on every parameter including fixtures, imports at the top, parametrize instead of loops, model_construct for fixtures. Run \`uv run pytest -q\` yourself and report anything failing or skipped. Look for tests that would pass even if the feature were broken.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'agent-ux',
    prompt: `Lens: what a calling LLM agent experiences. Read the five tool descriptions, the parameter descriptions, the server instructions, the README rows and the error messages as if you were an agent that has never seen this server: can you find the tool by search ('компоненты', 'component')? Do you know where a component id comes from, that lead is a login, what version does and when to omit it, that delete is permanent, how queue_get_metadata expand=components differs from queue_get_components? Is anything promised that the code does not do, or anything the code does that the caller is not told (an empty update refused, an update needing a read first)? Flag descriptions that are over budget or say nothing.`,
  },
]

const reviews = await parallel(LENSES.map(lens => () =>
  agent(
    `${COMMON}

You are reviewing the uncommitted implementation of the spec. Get the diff with \`git status\` and \`git diff\` (new files are untracked: read them directly, \`git status --short\` lists them). Do not edit any file. ${lens.prompt}

Report only defects you can point at (file, line, evidence), ranked by severity. No praise, no restating what is fine. An empty findings list is a valid answer.`,
    { label: `review:${lens.key}`, phase: 'Review', schema: FINDINGS_SCHEMA, effort: 'high' },
  )
))

const all = reviews.filter(Boolean).flatMap(r => r.findings)
log(`Review produced ${all.length} findings`)

phase('Verify')
const verified = await parallel(all.map((f, i) => () =>
  parallel([0, 1, 2].map(k => () =>
    agent(
      `${COMMON}

A reviewer claims the following defect in the uncommitted implementation (read the current files, \`git diff\`, and the spec to check it):

File: ${f.file}${f.line ? ':' + f.line : ''}
Title: ${f.title}
Claim: ${f.description}
Suggested fix: ${f.suggested_fix}

Try to REFUTE it${k === 0 ? ' from the code and tests' : k === 1 ? ' from the spec and the rules/ documents' : ' by asking whether it matters to a caller or a maintainer at all'}. It is refuted if the claim is false, already handled, contradicts the spec's decisions, or is a matter of taste with no rule behind it. Default to refuted=true when uncertain. Do not edit any file.`,
      { label: `verify:${i}:${k}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT_SCHEMA },
    )
  )).then(votes => {
    const real = votes.filter(Boolean).filter(v => !v.refuted).length >= 2
    return { ...f, real, votes: votes.filter(Boolean).map(v => v.reasoning) }
  })
))

const confirmed = verified.filter(Boolean).filter(f => f.real)
const rejected = verified.filter(Boolean).filter(f => !f.real)
log(`${confirmed.length} findings confirmed, ${rejected.length} refuted`)

phase('Fix')
let fix = null
if (confirmed.length > 0) {
  fix = await agent(
    `${COMMON}

The implementation is in the working tree, uncommitted. Apply the following confirmed review findings, keeping to the spec and the repository style. Where a finding contradicts the spec, the spec wins - skip it and say why. After the changes run \`task\` and make it pass; if a rule test fails, fix the cause, not the test.

${confirmed.map((f, i) => `${i + 1}. [${f.severity}] ${f.file}${f.line ? ':' + f.line : ''} - ${f.title}\n   ${f.description}\n   Fix: ${f.suggested_fix}`).join('\n\n')}

Return the structured report; task_output_tail is the last 40 lines of the final \`task\` run.`,
    { label: 'fix', phase: 'Fix', schema: FIX_SCHEMA, effort: 'high' },
  )
} else {
  log('Nothing confirmed, skipping the fix pass')
}

return { impl, confirmed, rejected, fix }

